    <!-- What Is GMT? -->
    <section class="content-section" style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
      <h2>What Is GMT?</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Greenwich Mean Time (GMT)</strong> is the historic solar time standard measured at the Royal Observatory in Greenwich, London, situated at 0° longitude (the Prime Meridian).
      </p>
      <p>
        GMT serves as the international reference benchmark for UTC+0. Although Coordinated Universal Time (UTC) has replaced GMT as the formal atomic time standard in technology and navigation, GMT remains the standard civil reference name in the UK and worldwide conversion software. You can learn more about GMT on our <a href="/blog/what-is-gmt/" style="color: var(--accent);">What Is GMT guide</a> or use our primary <a href="/" style="color: var(--accent);">GMT to EST Converter</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- What Is NST? -->
    <section class="content-section" style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
      <h2>What Is NST?</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Newfoundland Standard Time (NST)</strong> is the official standard time zone observed on the island of Newfoundland in the province of Newfoundland and Labrador, Canada.
      </p>
      <p>
        NST operates at <strong>UTC−3:30</strong> (Coordinated Universal Time minus 3 hours and 30 minutes). In international software systems (the IANA Time Zone Database), Newfoundland Standard Time is represented by the location string <code>America/St_Johns</code>.
      </p>
    </section>

