    <section class="content-section" style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
      <h2>Why NST Has a 30-Minute Offset</h2>
      <p>
        In 1935, prior to Newfoundland joining the Canadian Confederation in 1949, the Dominion of Newfoundland established its own official time zone. Because the island sits geographically between 52°W and 59°W longitude, standard noon solar time in St. John's lies approximately 3 hours and 30 minutes behind Greenwich longitude.
      </p>
      <p>
        To align civil time closer to natural solar daylight, Newfoundland chose UTC−3:30 instead of rounding up to Atlantic Time (UTC-4) or down to Greenland Time (UTC-3). Today, it remains one of North America's most distinctive half-hour time zones.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Why GMT to NST Sometimes Changes the Date -->
    <section class="content-section" style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
      <h2>Why GMT to NST Sometimes Changes the Date</h2>
      <p>
        Because NST is 3 hours 30 minutes behind GMT, early morning hours in GMT (from 00:00 to 03:29 GMT) correspond to the late evening hours of the <strong>previous calendar day</strong> in Newfoundland.
      </p>
      <ul style="padding-left: 1.2rem; line-height: 1.9;">
        <li><strong>00:00 GMT:</strong> Equals 20:30 (8:30 PM) NST on the <strong>PREVIOUS calendar day</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>02:00 GMT:</strong> Equals 22:30 (10:30 PM) NST on the <strong>PREVIOUS calendar day</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>03:29 GMT:</strong> Equals 23:59 (11:59 PM) NST on the <strong>PREVIOUS calendar day</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>03:30 GMT:</strong> Reaches 00:00 midnight NST on the <strong>SAME calendar day</strong>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        For instance, an automated database task running at 01:00 AM GMT on January 1 occurs at 9:30 PM NST on December 31 in Newfoundland.
      </p>
    </section>

